      "writtenOn": "2024-07-30T16:14:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "A few thoughts:\n- AuthException seems more consistent to me with the other ways we could run into the user not being permitted to create a ref. For example, if I run into this code path by running `git push`, I\u0027m not sure how a ResourceConflictException would show up in the client, versus a message saying that I was not allowed based on permissions would be clearer, I think\n- do we want any users to be permitted this? For example, should administrators be allowed? I\u0027m fine with \"no\" (in a YAGNI sense), just wanted to ask in case we have thoughts about that\n- in the same spirit: in the `forPush` case, `checkCreateCommit` calls `forRef.check(RefPermission.UPDATE)` to see if the caller is allowed to set the ref to an arbitrary value; in that scenario, do we want to permit this? We could use similar logic - for example,\n\n```java\n  if (!forPush) { // this shouldn\u0027t happen\n    throw new IllegalStateException(); // or whatever exception seems appropriate\n  }\n  forRef.check(RefPermission.UPDATE);\n```",
